#### In June, the Justice Department allowed CBS News and CNN to be owned by a single owner without any conditions. In October, the same department’s antitrust office opened an investigation into both of them and three other networks for acting together. If that sounds backward, it’s because it is.

I’ve spent the past three weeks on the Paramount–Warner Bros. merger. In [Who Actually Stops a $110 Billion Merger?](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/who-actually-stops-a-110-billion-merger/), I asked who was supposed to stop it. In [The Judge Said Her Court Wasn’t a Rubber Stamp](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/paramount-warner-merger-approved-judge-settlement/), I reported that nobody did. The deal closed October 6.

Three days later, the Justice Department’s antitrust office went after the newsrooms.

> **Sherman Act:** the 1890 federal antitrust law that bans agreements among competitors that unreasonably restrain trade, including some coordinated boycotts.

> **Press pool:** a small rotating group of reporters and camera crews who cover the president up close and share their footage and notes with every other outlet. The five-network TV pool is ABC, CBS, CNN, Fox News and NBC.

*Editorial: This Dispatch contains my opinion, clearly marked as such, alongside sourced reporting.*

###### What Happened

On September 18, President Trump announced he was banning CNN, MS NOW, and Politico from the White House. He said the outlets “shouldn’t be able to constantly write or report FICTION and LIES.” Government lawyers later argued national security.

The five networks that make up the TV pool, ABC, CBS, CNN, Fox News and NBC, responded by suspending pooled coverage of the president. In a joint statement, they said: “No administration should reject a news organization because it objects to its reporting.”

On September 24, U.S. District Judge Timothy Kelly ordered the White House to restore the three outlets’ access, and it did. On October 13, he extended that order for the length of the lawsuit, writing that the ban was “unlawful viewpoint discrimination.”

In between, on October 9, the Justice Department sent civil investigative demands, a kind of subpoena for documents, to all five networks. A department spokesperson said: “Group boycotts among commercial competitors can violate the Sherman Act.”

###### The Same Department, Four Months Apart

Here’s the part that should stop you cold.

**June 12, 2026:** the Justice Department’s Antitrust Division cleared Paramount’s roughly $110 billion purchase of Warner Bros. Discovery. As Variety reported, it required no divestitures, no behavioral remedies, no concessions. The department said the deal would actually increase competition. That deal put two Hollywood studios, two streaming services, and two national newsrooms, CBS News and CNN, under one owner, David Ellison.

**October 9, 2026:** The same division sent demands to CBS and CNN, along with three other networks, to investigate whether they illegally coordinated by suspending pool coverage for a few days in September.

So when CBS and CNN act together for a few days to protect a reporter’s access, the Justice Department calls it a possible antitrust violation. When CBS and CNN are permanently combined into one company, the Justice Department calls it good for competition.

Under the department’s own theory, coordination between CBS and CNN is the danger. As of October 6, it no longer even counts as coordination. They have the same owner. The Justice Department approved that in June.

###### What a Competent Justice Department Would Have Done

*Editorial:* A competent Justice Department, worried about two of the country’s largest news organizations acting in concert, had a tool for that, and it had the tool in June. It could have required Paramount to spin off CBS News and CNN as independent companies as a condition of the deal.

That isn’t radical. The department has required divestitures in media deals before. When Disney bought most of 21st Century Fox, the department required Disney to sell off 22 regional sports networks as a condition of approval. Connecticut Attorney General William Tong said the same thing about this deal: he wanted CNN and CBS News divested entirely. He didn’t get it. The state settlement settled for an editorial independence board instead, and Colorado and Washington wouldn’t even sign that part.

Instead, the department approved one owner for both newsrooms, asked for nothing, and is now investigating those newsrooms for standing up for a reporter. That isn’t antitrust enforcement. It’s either incompetence or something worse.

###### Who Started It

On October 14, Semafor reported, citing three people familiar with the matter, that the White House played a central role in the investigation. Two of them said the idea was first discussed inside the White House, which then pushed the Justice Department to pursue it.

Both deny it. A department official said “seasoned attorneys within the Justice Department, including the antitrust division,” came up with it first. A White House spokeswoman said, “Your sources have it wrong.”

Two more facts from the same report. The Antitrust Division’s former chief of staff, Sara Matar, joined the White House counsel’s office in August. And in April, Attorney General Todd Blanche said it is the president’s “right” to suggest who should be investigated.

Even Fox News, the network most friendly to this White House, is a target. That tells you this isn’t about which network’s coverage anyone likes. It’s about whether newsrooms are allowed to push back at all.

###### What the Experts Say

Former antitrust officials from both parties aren’t buying it. Bill Baer, who ran the Antitrust Division under President Obama, told the Washington Post: “It looks like total retaliation.” William Kovacic, whom President George W. Bush appointed chairman of the Federal Trade Commission, called the department’s legal justification “feeble” and said Trump’s own public attacks on the networks would undermine the case. “This will be fatal to the case,” he said.

There’s precedent on the networks’ side. In 1982, in *NAACP v. Claiborne Hardware*, the Supreme Court ruled that a peaceful, politically motivated boycott is protected by the First Amendment.

**In fairness:** antitrust law does apply to news organizations. The Supreme Court said so in 1945 in *Associated Press v. United States*. And not every boycott with a political flavor is protected; courts have ruled against groups whose boycotts were really about money. The department hasn’t filed a case, and it may never file one. But it doesn’t have to. Document demands are expensive, slow, and intimidating. Sometimes the investigation is the point.

###### The Pattern

This isn’t the first time the Antitrust Division has looked this way. Regular readers will remember HPE–Juniper, the merger in which two antitrust officials were fired for opposing the settlement. The division still has no Senate-confirmed leader. The president’s nominee to run it, Adam Candeub, was the top lawyer at the FCC that approved Skydance’s purchase of Paramount in 2025. The Senate left town until after the election without voting on him.

An antitrust office is supposed to protect competition so that consumers and citizens have choices. This one cleared the biggest media merger in years without conditions, and now it’s aiming its subpoena power at the newsrooms within it.

###### Call to Action

1. **Watch the court case.** Judge Kelly’s order keeps CNN, MS NOW and Politico in the White House for now. An appeal could change that.
1. **Ask your senators one question.** Before any vote on Candeub, will the nominee commit that antitrust enforcement will never be used to punish news coverage?
1. **Support an independent press, in whatever form you read it.** The fewer owners there are, the more each newsroom’s independence matters.
1. **Read the background.** [Who Actually Stops a $110 Billion Merger?](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/who-actually-stops-a-110-billion-merger/) has the full record on how this merger got through.

The Justice Department could have protected the public from one company owning two national newsrooms. It chose not to. Now it wants to know why those newsrooms stood together for a few days. The answer should have been obvious: somebody had to.

